    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Cubic Polynomial Given Three Real Roots and a Leading Coefficient",
    "problem": "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=1,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-2.$ Find $P(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{1}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=1,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-2(x-(-5))(x-0)(x-1) \\\\  & =-2x(x+5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x(x+5)(x-1) & =-2x({x}^{2}+4x-5) \\\\  & =-2({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+10x.$",
